# What may enter the build context, stated as an allowlist.
#
# The Dockerfile assembles the image with a single `COPY . <workdir>`, so this
# file -- and nothing else -- decides what lands in that immutable layer. It
# used to hold one line, `**/.git`, which meant every path that happened to be
# on the builder's disk was baked in: `config/local.*` and `config/runtime.json`
# (a live session secret and runtime state), `config/development.*` and
# `config/production.*` deployment config, `node_modules`, the 435 MB
# `public/components` tree, the generated CSS, and any workstation residue
# sitting alongside them.
#
# A deny-list is not sufficient here, and that is a measurement rather than a
# preference: this checkout carries an untracked directory (`blitzy/`) that
# `.gitignore` does not name, so a .dockerignore that merely mirrored
# `.gitignore` would still have copied it in. Only an allowlist makes the
# default "excluded", so residue and secrets cannot enter a layer by accident
# and a path nobody has thought about yet needs no new rule to stay out.
#
# The one maintenance cost of this shape, written down because it is invisible
# otherwise: A NEW TRACKED TOP-LEVEL ENTRY MUST BE RE-ADMITTED IN SECTION 2
# BELOW OR IT WILL NOT REACH THE IMAGE. The re-admission list is the output of
# `git ls-files | cut -d/ -f1 | sort -u`, so that command is also how it is
# checked. A new file inside an already-admitted directory needs no edit.
#
# Docker matches these patterns against the whole context-relative path with Go
# path matching, where a single `*` does not cross a `/`. Two consequences are
# load-bearing below: a single-segment `*` excludes only top-level entries, so
# re-admitting a directory re-admits everything under it (which `**` would not
# do), and the last pattern that matches a path wins, so the three sections
# have to stay in this order.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Exclude every top-level entry, including dotfiles and untracked residue.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Re-admit the tracked top-level entries, and only those.
#
# Deliberately the complete tracked set rather than a hand-picked minimum: it
# keeps the image's contents identical to what the previous whole-checkout COPY
# produced apart from the residue this file exists to keep out, which is the
# smallest change that fixes the problem. Pruning tracked source would be a
# separate decision with its own risk.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!.bowerrc
!.dockerignore
!.gitignore
!.nvmrc
!CHANGELOG.md
!CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
!COMPONENTS.md
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!Dockerfile
!GETTING_STARTED.md
!LICENSE
!README.md
!app.js
!catalog-info.yaml
!config
!docker-compose.yml
!docs
!lib
!mkdocs.yml
!package-lock.json
!package.json
!public
!scripts
!serverside
!static
!test
!vite.config.mjs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Re-exclude the ignored, generated and secret-bearing paths that live
#    inside the directories section 2 just re-admitted.
#
# Section 2 pulls whole directories back in, so everything here would otherwise
# return with them. `**/` prefixes reach any depth, matching the unanchored
# `.gitignore` entries they correspond to; paths without one are anchored at the
# context root exactly as their `.gitignore` counterparts are.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Nested repository metadata, at any depth.
**/.git

# Local, runtime and deployment configuration -- the secret-bearing half of this
# file's purpose. The extensions are enumerated one by one on purpose: a
# `config/local.*` or `config/production.*` wildcard would also match the
# TRACKED `config/local.example.yaml` and `config/production.yaml.dist`, because
# `*` happily matches "example.yaml" and "yaml.dist". Those two are build inputs
# and stay in the context.
config/local.yaml
config/local.yml
config/local.json
config/runtime.json
config/runtime.json.tmp-*
config/development.yaml
config/development.yml
config/development.json
config/production.yaml
config/production.yml
config/production.json
config/migration.json
config/migrationDb.json

# Installed dependencies, at any depth: the root install is performed by
# `npm ci` inside the image, and each serverside unit installs its own, so a
# host tree copied in would only shadow them with the wrong platform's binaries.
**/node_modules

# Fetched and generated frontend assets. `scripts/fetch-components.js` publishes
# public/components inside the image and `npm run build:css` writes
# public/css/base.css and public/css/embed.css there, so copies from the host are
# stale by construction.
#
# Note the shapes here, because two of them are one character apart:
# public/css/base.css and public/css/embed.css are the two generated files,
# while public/css/challenge.css and everything under public/css/embed/ are
# tracked assets. The literal pattern `public/css/embed.css` cannot match
# `public/css/embed/embed.css`, which has a `/` where the pattern has a `.`, so
# the tracked assets stay in the context -- verified by listing the context.
public/components
public/vendor
public/_published
public/404.html
public/50x.html
public/maintenance.html
public/js/dist
public/css/base.css
public/css/base.css.map
public/css/embed.css
public/css/embed.css.map
public/css/publish
lib/views/courses/download/course.css
lib/views/courses/download/course.css.map

# Migration backups written next to the migrations they came from.
lib/migrations/backups

# Build and editor residue. The top-level forms are already excluded by section
# 1; these reach the nested copies, matching the unanchored `.gitignore` rules.
**/.sass-cache
**/npm-debug.log
**/build.log
**/test-*.js
**/.DS_Store
**/*.swp
**/*.pyc
**/*.sublime-project
**/*.sublime-workspace
**/Vagrantfile
**/.vagrant
**/.agignore
